#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158012} :activity/id #uuid "d786bc7e-7381-59dc-826e-1b785938d470" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050777 :topic/id #uuid "65e4f73e-43a8-5089-914d-135e7396b995" :topic/math-academy-id 2821} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158012 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158579 :tutorial/content "Two statements are **logically equivalent** if they have identical truth values in their respective truth tables. In this lesson, we'll use truth tables to show that two complex compound statements are logically equivalent. \n\nIf two statements $P$ and $Q$ are logically equivalent, we write\n\n$$\nP\\equiv Q\n$$\n\nwhere the symbol \"$\\equiv$\" means \"is logically equivalent to.\"\n\nFor example, let's use a truth table to prove the following logical equivalence:\n\n$$\n(A \\land \\neg A) \\lor B \\equiv B\n$$\n\nFirst, we construct truth tables with columns $A$ and $B$ containing all possible combinations of truth values. Then, we create another column for $\\neg A,$ which has the opposite truth values to $A.$\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n\nNow, we add a column for $A \\land \\neg A.$ Recall that $A\\land \\neg A$ is true if $A$ is true and $\\neg A$ is true and is false otherwise. In this case, $A\\land \\neg A$ is always false.\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\,$ | $\\, A \\land \\neg A \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n\nFinally, we complete the table with a column for $(A \\land \\neg A) \\lor B.$ Recall that $(A \\land \\neg A) \\lor B$ is true if $(A \\land \\neg A)$ is true, or $B$ is true, or both are true. \n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\,$ | $\\, A \\land \\neg A \\,$ | $\\, (A \\land \\neg A) \\lor B \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n\nWe see that the truth values of $(A \\land \\neg A) \\lor B$ are the same as the truth values of $B.$ This proves that $(A \\land \\neg A) \\lor B$ is logically equivalent to $B.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "52050e7d-e6dd-5b8e-a1f9-56a8c00bbaa7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8260 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c35dc71e-cbb8-5b1e-9e21-1319c9031864" :step/math-academy-id 120201 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158013}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158013 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157229 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157010 :question/id #uuid "1901808f-9cd7-52c2-85a7-8937aa8d4da3" :question/math-academy-id "e-13222"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb27ab3a-0ca0-5e64-94ce-24a393d86f64" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048349}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157633} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571425}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Truth Table"} :step/id #uuid "c619f370-2162-5e52-83a2-7a22517f0f26" :step/math-academy-id 120202 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158014}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158014 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157230 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157011 :question/id #uuid "beb3d6c7-1c43-5d6b-a817-e1756ea36818" :question/math-academy-id "e-13223"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a6a36e8-e14e-5051-acae-48de63ff3eff" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157634} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381611} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571423}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalent Statements Using Partially Completed Truth Tables"} :step/id #uuid "5765c4ca-7ca3-542f-b314-a0b02c46501b" :step/math-academy-id 12